WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:03.529
Film Courage: How does someone who wants to
be a professional screenwriter approach it

00:00:03.529 --> 00:00:05.100
like a professional screenwriter.

00:00:05.100 --> 00:00:06.460
What is their mindset?

00:00:06.460 --> 00:00:07.750
What is their time like?

00:00:07.750 --> 00:00:12.099
What are they doing that is different from
someone who says “I want to be a writer”

00:00:12.099 --> 00:00:13.590
and both have equal talent?

00:00:13.590 --> 00:00:17.120
But one’s approach and mindset is different
from the other one that “Ahh…I want to

00:00:17.120 --> 00:00:18.850
be a writer” but doesn’t do that.

00:00:18.850 --> 00:00:19.850
Scott Myers, DePaul University Professor/Screenwriter/Creator
Gointothestory.blcklst(dot)com: That’s a

00:00:19.850 --> 00:00:20.850
really good question.

00:00:20.850 --> 00:00:32.370
I had one of my students is now a very successful
TV runner and soon to be directing a movie

00:00:32.370 --> 00:00:36.300
that she wrote, Lisa Joy.

00:00:36.300 --> 00:00:42.480
She was co-executive producer of WESTWORLD
and she came to a class that I taught at Westwood

00:00:42.480 --> 00:00:47.010
for a weekend seminar and that question came
up and they posed that question to her.

00:00:47.010 --> 00:00:52.100
And she said “Well I was working as a lawyer
and before that I was going to law school.”

00:00:52.100 --> 00:00:55.230
And the way I approached it was I am going
to act like I’m a professional screenwriter.

00:00:55.230 --> 00:00:59.000
I am going to approach this like a job.”

00:00:59.000 --> 00:01:02.360
And so obviously how do you do this in terms
of hours.

00:01:02.360 --> 00:01:04.150
But set that aside, that’s the mentality
they have.

00:01:04.150 --> 00:01:08.299
You have to really immerse yourself in it.

00:01:08.299 --> 00:01:12.280
It’s not just about writing the script,
it’s about learning the craft.

00:01:12.280 --> 00:01:16.580
And so one of those things and I came up with
them years ago for my blog which is watch

00:01:16.580 --> 00:01:19.810
movies, read scripts, write pages.

00:01:19.810 --> 00:01:21.820
Those three things are absolutely essential.

00:01:21.820 --> 00:01:25.790
You’ve got to watch movies sometimes you
just kind of kick back and enjoy it.

00:01:25.790 --> 00:01:30.110
But then there are other times where you stop
it scene-by-scene and you write a scene-by-scene

00:01:30.110 --> 00:01:34.790
breakdown and you are looking for where the
plot line points land, the major events that

00:01:34.790 --> 00:01:38.780
push the plot into a new direction, you are
looking at subplots, and those little mini

00:01:38.780 --> 00:01:42.689
stories (m-i-n-i stories) and how they play
into the ‘A’ story.

00:01:42.689 --> 00:01:46.409
You’re looking at characters and their transformational
arc.

00:01:46.409 --> 00:01:50.650
So there are watching movies and sometimes
you do it in a very rigorous fashion.

00:01:50.650 --> 00:01:52.130
Then reading scripts similarly.

00:01:52.130 --> 00:01:57.060
You can just kind of sit there and go through
script and enjoy it but then also do the same

00:01:57.060 --> 00:01:58.670
thing, you can do a scene-by-scene breakdown.

00:01:58.670 --> 00:02:02.840
You’re doing the same sort of analysis that
you would if you were watching a movie.

00:02:02.840 --> 00:02:07.410
Reading scripts is absolutely critical, particularly
scripts written in the last five years because

00:02:07.410 --> 00:02:12.090
the style changes constantly and there will
be people who tell you well you can’t do

00:02:12.090 --> 00:02:13.590
this or you can’t do that.

00:02:13.590 --> 00:02:21.750
It’s much more important for you to read
scripts where you are seeing how professionals

00:02:21.750 --> 00:02:25.980
approach the writing and you are then taking
that knowledge and bringing it to bear on

00:02:25.980 --> 00:02:27.730
your own writing.

00:02:27.730 --> 00:02:30.660
And then of course writing pages.

00:02:30.660 --> 00:02:36.280
I have this thing that I came up with on my
blog that seems quite popular which is 1 - 2

00:02:36.280 --> 00:02:37.580
-7- 14.

00:02:37.580 --> 00:02:39.680
One is read one script a week.

00:02:39.680 --> 00:02:42.550
Two is watch two movies per week.

00:02:42.550 --> 00:02:45.140
Seven is write one page a day (7 pages).

00:02:45.140 --> 00:02:50.400
And 14 is fourteen hours a week (basically
two hours a day) you should be researching

00:02:50.400 --> 00:02:54.970
another project, you are rewriting a project,
you are doing character development, you are

00:02:54.970 --> 00:03:00.080
doing story development, you are generating
and assessing story ideas, you are tracking

00:03:00.080 --> 00:03:02.459
trends, you are reading the trades.

00:03:02.459 --> 00:03:06.390
If you want to do this, if you really want
to do this, you have to understand that you

00:03:06.390 --> 00:03:10.599
are competing against people who are very
serious-minded and have a lot of talent.

00:03:10.599 --> 00:03:15.550
It’s not easy in fact the odds against success
are just enormous.

00:03:15.550 --> 00:03:18.610
But the way to do it is to approach it like
you’re a professional.

00:03:18.610 --> 00:03:25.900
And so if you can carve out 20 hours a week
(let’s say 16 hours a week, 2 hours a day)

00:03:25.900 --> 00:03:32.110
as opposed to going and eating lunch with
your buddies for an hour and a half.

00:03:32.110 --> 00:03:37.620
Well no…use that time to actually do some
work as a writer.

00:03:37.620 --> 00:03:42.170
You need to learn the craft and then exhibit
what you’ve learned on the page so that

00:03:42.170 --> 00:03:47.040
when you’ve written that script, it speaks
to someone.

00:03:47.040 --> 00:03:48.879
Someone will get it and see that this is a
professional writer.

